/* Pass 16B - source-complete voice corrections to eight existing crossover chapters. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const updates={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-12":{"number":12,"title":"The Dragon's Breath","content":"The dragon's breath was the name of a tea Iroh served only when he had made a difficult decision.\n\nIt was black, smoky, and strong enough to make my eyes water. He poured it into two cups after the morning rush, when the shop had finally quieted.\n\n“This seems hostile,” I said.\n\n“It is honest.”\n\n“You keep using that word to describe things that taste like ash.”\n\n“Some truths are not sweet.”\n\nI lifted the cup. “What did you decide?”\n\nIroh sat across from me. The letter from the Fire Nation lay unopened beside the tea.\n\n“I will go,” he said.\n\nThe room seemed to tilt.\n\n“For how long?”\n\n“Three weeks at first. Perhaps longer if the negotiations require it.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“The western provinces. Then the capital.”\n\nI held the cup between my hands. The heat was almost painful.\n\n“You don’t have to look so calm,” Iroh said.\n\n“I’m trying not to make this about me.”\n\n“It is about you. It is also about many other things.”\n\n“You cannot be responsible for all of them.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\nHis mouth tightened. “I am learning.”\n\nI took a sip of the tea and immediately regretted it.\n\nIroh's eyes warmed. “You need not finish it.”\n\n“I am committed to the lesson.”\n\n“Pride is a poor seasoning.”\n\n“You made the tea.”\n\n“And I am willing to admit it is unpleasant.”\n\nI laughed despite the ache in my chest.\n\nIroh reached across the table. “I will write.”\n\n“Letters?”\n\n“Letters.”\n\n“Not just formal reports.”\n\n“I promise to include at least one complaint about the food.”\n\n“And one thing you are actually feeling.”\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n“Iroh.”\n\n“I will try.”\n\n“That is not the same as promise.”\n\n“I promise to try honestly.”\n\nI accepted that.\n\nThe days before his departure became a strange mixture of work and preparation. Iroh arranged for another tea master to oversee the shop. I learned which suppliers were reliable and which ones sold leaves that had been stored too near fish.\n\nMina found the revised rota on the counter and read it twice.\n\n\"Who put me on closing duty every night?\" she asked.\n\nIroh looked up from his travel papers. \"I thought the extra wages might be welcome.\"\n\n\"Wages are welcome. Seven late shifts in a row are not.\"\n\nHe folded the paper. That was when I realized he'd planned a tea shop with the efficiency of a former general and the imagination of a man who did not have to stay behind and scrub its kettles.\n\n\"How many people have agreed to this?\" Mina asked.\n\n\"Not enough,\" I said.\n\nIroh reached for a pen, then stopped. \"I was going to ask when the plan was finished.\"\n\n\"You can ask before you finish,\" she said. \"It saves ink.\"\n\nShe crossed out four evenings and penciled in the other tea master's actual hours. She could handle the accounts on Tuesday, but not a delivery at dawn as well. Iroh would have to find a runner or shorten the menu.\n\n\"Shorten the menu?\" He sounded deeply offended.\n\n\"The world will survive without the smoked plum blend for three days.\"\n\n\"I am less certain I will.\"\n\nWhen Mina returned to the front room, he looked at the rota for a long time. His finger rested on the blank lines.\n\n\"This isn't an army,\" I said.\n\nThe words came out too sharply, and his smile disappeared. I didn't take them back.\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"And this is not a siege to be won by careful arrangements made over other people's heads.\"\n\nHe rewrote the schedule with empty spaces where workers could choose their shifts. Then he sealed the first of his letters to the Fire Nation. It took him longer than he'd spent drawing the route, perhaps because this time the people whose lives he was arranging could speak back.\n\n“Would you oversee the accounts while I am gone?” Iroh asked.\n\n“That sounds like an error.”\n\n“It is a calculated risk.”\n\n“You have known me for less than a month.”\n\n“I have known you for long enough to see that you notice what others overlook.”\n\n“You are going to make me responsible for the kettle.”\n\n“Only the kettle with a temper.”\n\n“You are leaving me with your most difficult employee.”\n\n“You are the most difficult employee.”\n\n“I have never threatened to whistle at you.”\n\n“The kettle has never threatened to leave.”\n\nThe joke faded between us.\n\nOn the last evening, I climbed to the balcony with two cups of tea. The city stretched below, lanterns appearing one by one.\n\n“Are you afraid?” Iroh asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Of what?”\n\n“That you’ll realize the world needs you more than I do.”\n\n“The world needs many things. It does not decide whom I love.”\n\n“You could meet someone.”\n\n“I could.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“That was not helpful,” he admitted.\n\n“No.”\n\n“But it is true that I cannot promise nothing will ever change. I can promise that I will not make decisions about us in silence.”\n\n“You will write.”\n\n“I will write.”\n\n“You will tell me when you are lonely.”\n\n“I will tell you.”\n\n“You will not turn yourself into a weapon just because people expect it.”\n\nIroh looked toward the city wall. “I will try.”\n\n“Honestly.”\n\n“Honestly.”\n\nI sat beside him. The balcony railing was cool under my hand.\n\n“What do you want to be when you come back?” I asked.\n\nHe thought about it.\n\n“The man who owns a tea shop,” he said. “The man who knows which blend you prefer. The man who has a room above the shop and someone who complains about his breakfast.”\n\n“You do not have to become smaller to be loved.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You can be both.”\n\n“Both what?”\n\n“The person who helps, and the person who rests. The man people call when they need wisdom, and the man who wants to sleep in late.”\n\nIroh smiled. “I would like to sleep in late.”\n\n“You can start when you return.”\n\n“That is a generous offer.”\n\n“Do not mistake it for permission to stop making tea.”\n\nHe laughed, then leaned toward me.\n\n“May I kiss you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nWe shared a quiet kiss, letting the coming separation sit between us without turning it into a promise either of us could not keep.\n\nHe paused when my fingers tightened in his coat.\n\n“Still comfortable?”\n\n“I am sad.”\n\n“That was not my question.”\n\n“Yes. I am comfortable.”\n\nHe kissed me again, more deeply. The city lights trembled below. When the cold finally drove us inside, neither of us pretended the leaving had become easier.\n\nWe stayed at the table until the tea went cold, talking through travel routes, people he meant to find, and all the practical details that were easier to name than goodbye. Every so often one of us made a joke and the other let it work for a minute. It was not enough to make the separation harmless. It was enough to keep it human.\n\nBefore dawn, Iroh left a letter beneath my cup.\n\nIt contained only one sentence: I WILL COME BACK TO THE TEA.\n\nI folded it carefully and put it beside my bed.\n\nThe morning after his decision, Iroh woke before me and tried to move quietly. He failed when the floorboard beside the bed complained.\n\n“The room is betraying me,” he said.\n\nI opened one eye. “The room has always been honest.”\n\n“It has chosen a poor time.”\n\nHe sat on the edge of the bed. His travel bag rested near the door. Seeing it made the room feel smaller.\n\n“You are allowed to change your mind,” I said.\n\n“About leaving?”\n\n“About anything.”\n\nIroh looked at the bag. “I know. That is why the choice is difficult.”\n\n“If you stay, you may be accused of selfishness.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“If you go, you may be accused of abandoning me.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“People are going to accuse you either way.”\n\nHe smiled faintly. “You have found the center of the problem.”\n\nI sat up. “Then choose what you can live with.”\n\n“I am choosing to help people who have no other advocate.”\n\n“And you are choosing to come back.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then I will trust the choices, not the distance between them.”\n\nIroh's hand found mine.\n\n“You are making this easier,” he said.\n\n“I am not trying to.”\n\n“That is why it helps.”\n\nDownstairs, the shop needed to be prepared for his absence. I wrote instructions in his careful handwriting because my own letters were too large for the narrow page. The substitute tea master complained about the kettle. I told her that the kettle was now her responsibility.\n\nShe looked horrified.\n\n“It is only a kettle,” I said.\n\n“That is what he said before it attacked him.”\n\nIroh packed a second letter for Zuko. He folded it three times before sealing it.\n\n“Will he be angry?” I asked.\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“Will he understand?”\n\n“Eventually.”\n\n“You sound certain.”\n\n“I am certain he loves me. Understanding may take longer.”\n\nI watched him press the seal into the wax.\n\n“Do you want him to visit the shop?”\n\n“I want him to visit because he wants to, not because I have created another obligation.”\n\n“You worry about that a lot.”\n\n“Obligation can look very much like love when people are frightened.”\n\nI touched his wrist. “I will not stay because I owe you.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I will not come because I owe you.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I am coming because I want to see the road.”\n\n“And the governors?”\n\n“I want to see you argue with them.”\n\n“That is a very specific desire.”\n\n“You are impressive when you are angry.”\n\nIroh laughed. “I will try to be impressive responsibly.”\n\nThe day passed in preparations. Customers brought food for the journey. The old pai sho players gave Iroh a tile they claimed was lucky. The woman with the bright scarves left a bundle of dried flowers and told me not to let him forget to sleep.\n\n“Everyone is giving me instructions,” Iroh said.\n\n“Now you know how I feel.”\n\nHe kissed my temple.\n\nThat evening, I made the dragon's breath tea. I did not like it any better, but I understood why he chose it.\n\n“It tastes like a decision,” I said.\n\n“Some decisions are not sweet.”\n\n“You have said that too.”\n\n“It remains true.”\n\nI lifted my cup. “Then to difficult truth.”\n\nIroh touched his cup to mine. “And to returning.”\n\nLater, before departure, we shared one last kiss and sat together until it was time to sleep.\n\nAt dawn, he left the letter beneath my cup.\n\nI carried it against my heart while I walked beside him into the rain.\n\nAt the gate, Iroh stopped and adjusted the strap of my pack.\n\n“You are certain?” he asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You may still turn back.”\n\n“Ask me tomorrow.”\n\nHis smile was small and relieved.\n\nTogether, Iroh and I stepped onto the road.\n\nBehind me, the tea shop waited with its lamps dark and its door locked. Ahead, the rain softened the distance.\n\nIroh kept my hand in his.\n\nThe rain continued around me, gentle enough to endure.\n\nThe city disappeared behind the mist.\n\nThe road opened ahead.\n\nIroh adjusted the pack on his shoulder and offered me his hand. I took it without hesitation.","word_count":1866},"avatar-37":{"number":37,"title":"The Day We Return","content":"We took flowers and a kettle up the hill.\n\nIt had been a year since our first visit together, and I was still capable of worrying I would do the wrong thing. I carried the cups in a padded basket while Iroh carried the flowers, stopping once to fuss over a crooked ribbon that no one besides him would have noticed.\n\n\"You know it doesn't have to look perfect,\" I said.\n\n\"I know. But it ought to look as though I tried.\"\n\nAt the last bend, we found that a family had already laid flowers near the path. Their small offering had tipped in the wind. Iroh set our things down and straightened it, then stepped back before touching anything else.\n\n\"I wonder whether they would mind,\" he said.\n\n\"We could leave the rest alone.\"\n\nHe nodded. There were still ways of honoring somebody that did not require making decisions for strangers.\n\nThe city looked different from up here. New roofs caught the morning light. A repaired stretch of street ran toward the outer district. A building that had stood empty the previous year now had smoke rising from its chimney. Ba Sing Se had not become innocent. It was rebuilding unevenly, with people who argued about who had been helped first.\n\nIroh placed Lu Ten's flowers beside the stone.\n\n\"Last year I was afraid to tell you about his laugh,\" he said.\n\n\"I remember.\"\n\n\"Do you remember what I told you?\"\n\n\"That he laughed like he couldn't stop even if he wanted to. And that you tried very hard not to laugh with him.\"\n\n\"I failed spectacularly.\"\n\nI smiled. \"I always liked that part.\"\n\nHe poured tea into the offering bowl. He had brought the blend Lu Ten preferred, although it was no longer fashionable and Mina had had to ask an older merchant to help find the dried leaves.\n\nI had wanted to make the day easier. I nearly suggested bringing music, then remembered that easier for me and better for him weren't always the same thing.\n\n\"Would you like me to stay with you for a while, or walk down and give you privacy?\" I asked.\n\n\"Stay for the tea.\"\n\n\"After that?\"\n\n\"Ask me then.\"\n\nIt was such a simple answer I almost laughed at myself for wanting one that covered the whole day.\n\nWe sat with our backs to a sun-warmed stone. Iroh told a story about Lu Ten trying to teach him a children's board game and discovering that his father was less talented at simple rules than military strategy.\n\n\"He accused me of cheating,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Were you?\"\n\n\"I still maintain that the written instructions were ambiguous.\"\n\n\"So yes.\"\n\n\"An outrageous interpretation.\"\n\nI laughed. Then he did, and the sound rang out across the hill before it settled into silence.\n\nA little later, Iroh spoke of the war. He didn't ask me to make his part in it gentle. He knew the siege had broken lives that did not belong to his family. He had begun supporting a local relief kitchen, and he wanted me to see the accounts—not as proof he had earned forgiveness, but because the people doing the work should be properly paid.\n\n\"Can we stop there on our way back?\" I asked.\n\n\"If the workers are not too busy.\"\n\nWe had talked often about how my relationships were openly non-exclusive, and how I preferred polyamory because I wanted affection chosen rather than ranked. Today I thought about how different kinds of love could stand beside each other without having to explain themselves away. I couldn't compare the way I cared for Iroh to the love he carried for his son. I didn't want to.\n\n\"I used to think returning here would mean you were stuck,\" I said.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I think it means the day still belongs to him in some way.\"\n\n\"And also to us?\"\n\n\"If you want.\"\n\nHe held out his hand. \"I do.\"\n\nThe flower seller on the road down recognized Iroh and offered to refuse payment. He bought two small bunches at the listed price anyway.\n\n\"You've helped our family,\" the seller said.\n\n\"Then let me help you keep your business,\" Iroh answered, counting out the coins.\n\nThe seller looked pleased. I tucked the flowers into the basket for the shop and watched Iroh glance back toward the hill. He'd spoken of Lu Ten without trying to turn the boy into a saint, and of his part in the war without expecting gratitude for having changed. Both things had left him exhausted.\n\n\"Do you want to stop for a while before the relief kitchen?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. The work is important.\"\n\n\"Is the work more important than what you need?\"\n\nHe looked at me. \"Ah.\"\n\n\"That wasn't a trick question.\"\n\n\"It sounded suspiciously like one.\"\n\nWe sat under a tree where we could see the kitchen doors opening and closing. Workers carried sacks of grain inside; somebody laughed at a joke I couldn't hear. Iroh watched them.\n\n\"I was once very good at giving orders,\" he said. \"I'm afraid that when I give money now, people may hear an order hidden inside it.\"\n\n\"Then ask what they want, and believe them when they say no.\"\n\n\"Very tiresome wisdom.\"\n\n\"Learned from a terrible teacher.\"\n\nHe smiled. \"I accept the criticism.\"\n\nWhen we finally entered the kitchen, the cook who managed its supplies asked us to wait until the inventory was complete. Iroh agreed, even though his face suggested he was itching to help. I held his sleeve for a moment.\n\nThe cook's name was Jun. She had a burn across one wrist and the brisk impatience of a person who had already been interrupted six times before lunch.\n\nIroh glanced at a row of half-empty rice bins. \"Your western supplier is late.\"\n\n\"Three days,\" Jun said. \"Do you have rice?\"\n\n\"I can arrange some.\"\n\n\"I asked if you have it now.\"\n\nHe opened his mouth, closed it and gave an almost embarrassed laugh. \"No.\"\n\n\"Then help me carry the cups. Don't rearrange my kitchen.\"\n\nThe instruction was so plain that I wanted to laugh. Iroh put down his flowers, took the clean cups and followed her to the serving window. His hands knew exactly how to pour, but he was not in charge of this room.\n\nA young man waiting for food recognized him and asked whether he had been the general who once marched toward Ba Sing Se.\n\nIroh's fingers tightened around the cup. \"Yes.\"\n\nThe man took his portion and did not thank him. Jun served the next customer without asking anyone to turn the exchange into a lesson.\n\nIroh remained at the window until the agreed break. He had no grand wisdom to offer when we stepped outside. He only said, \"I remember how many kitchens an army can empty.\"\n\nI could not find a comforting answer that would make that history smaller. I took his hand and waited with him under the awning, smelling boiled rice and rain. When Jun called for the cups, he went back because it was the work she had actually asked him to do.\n\nAfterward he bought a new sack from a merchant who had some stock. He paid the price without asking that his name appear anywhere.\n\n\"We've talked about loving people without keeping score,\" I said quietly. \"I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I prefer polyamory. That doesn't make grief a competition either. You can miss Lu Ten and want me here. Neither thing needs to win.\"\n\nHis eyes closed for a moment. \"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Now please don't offer to wash every dish in the building.\"\n\n\"I was going to offer to wash only half.\"\n\n\"Generous restraint.\"\n\nHe leaned down, almost close enough to kiss me, but then someone called from the kitchen and we moved aside. Later, when the work was finished, he asked whether he could kiss me in the back courtyard. I said yes. It was brief, warm and a little awkward because he was still carrying the flowers.\n\nI laughed. He did too.\n\nThe flowers went into a vase on the shop's long table when we returned. The accounts were still waiting. A memorial day could include both love and work without turning either into an apology for the other.\n\nWe walked back together. At the relief kitchen, the cook who managed the funds showed us a ledger full of names and costs. A sack of grain had arrived short. Iroh offered to make up the difference from his personal money; the cook thanked him but insisted the supplier receive a written complaint as well. Nobody should have to depend on private generosity for a shipment they'd already paid for.\n\nI carried the complaint back to the shop so Mina could send it with the morning deliveries. Iroh put fresh water on for customers. Life resumed without asking whether grief had been completed.\n\nAs we finished closing, I found the little ribbon from the flower basket caught on his sleeve.\n\n\"Still crooked,\" I said.\n\nHe looked down. \"Terrible craftsmanship.\"\n\nI straightened it. He leaned close but waited.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\n\"Very much.\"\n\nThe kiss was brief, warm, and not a cure for anything. Upstairs, we sat together until the kettle emptied, then turned out the lamp.\n\nThe next morning, Mina asked why there were flowers pressed between two pages of the shop notebook.\n\n\"Because we wanted to remember them,\" Iroh said.\n\nShe pointed to the empty stock column beneath the petals. \"Then remember to order more jasmine.\"\n\nHe laughed and found the pen. The date stayed on the page. So did tomorrow's work.","word_count":1603}},"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-16":{"number":16,"title":"When the Van Breaks Down","content":"The van broke down again in front of the bakery, which Greg claimed was an improvement because at least the pastries were nearby.\n\nThe engine made one sharp cough and went silent. Greg turned the key twice more. Nothing happened.\n\n“It’s thinking,” he said.\n\n“It’s dead.”\n\n“It’s considering its options.”\n\n“It has chosen death.”\n\nGreg rested his head against the steering wheel. “The van has a flair for drama.”\n\nI climbed out and opened the hood. The belt looked worse than it had on the cow road. A dark stain spread beneath the engine.\n\n“You said the last repair was mostly compatible.”\n\n“It was compatible with optimism.”\n\n“That is not a mechanical category.”\n\n“It should be.”\n\nThe mechanic quoted Greg a price that made his shoulders tense. The van needed more than a belt. It needed patience, money, and a part that had not been manufactured in this century.\n\n“We can sell it,” Greg said afterward.\n\nI stood beside him on the sidewalk. “You don’t want to.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then don’t.”\n\n“It’s expensive.”\n\n“So is every object you love.”\n\nHe gave me a wounded look.\n\n“That was affectionate,” I said.\n\n“I’m not sure my wallet understands affection.”\n\nThe mechanic offered them a temporary ride. Greg declined. He called a friend instead, and the two of us walked to the car wash while waiting.\n\nThe friend was a regular customer who owed Greg precisely nothing. He still arrived with a pickup and a toolbox, mainly because he'd once promised to show Greg how to test a bad ground wire.\n\nGreg tried to pay him in pastries.\n\n\"That's not how I pay rent, man,\" the friend said.\n\n\"Could be. It's a delicious system.\"\n\nHe took the cash Greg offered instead. The engine still needed parts, and the mechanic had given us a written estimate. Greg stared at the amount for so long that I could almost hear a sad chord gathering behind his teeth.\n\n\"Maybe I can keep using the van for short trips,\" he said.\n\n\"Not until the mechanic clears it.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I know. I was trying to negotiate with a piece of paper.\"\n\nSteven phoned while we were at the car wash. Greg answered with exaggerated cheer, then admitted the van was in the shop. I heard Steven offer to help; Greg went quiet, listening. At last he said, \"You can give me a ride to the parts store on Friday if you're free. That's all. I can cover the repair.\"\n\nHe put the phone down and tapped the edge of the estimate in a rhythm he hadn't played before.\n\n\"What?\" I asked.\n\n\"Nothing. Maybe a song about a car that likes its mechanic better than its owner.\"\n\n\"Give it a tragic bridge.\"\n\n\"Aw, come on. I'm already paying for the transmission of emotional damage.\"\n\nI laughed. The friend was still waiting for a signature on the receipt. Greg signed it instead of writing another verse, then put the appointment on the calendar. It wasn't an inspiring solution. It was a real one.\n\n“I can pay for part of the repair,” I said.\n\nGreg stopped. “No.”\n\n“You didn’t let me finish.”\n\n“I know where this is going.”\n\n“You don’t.”\n\n“I know I love you, and I know I don’t want you feeling responsible for keeping my life intact.”\n\n“I’m not responsible for your life. I’m your partner.”\n\nThe word stopped him.\n\n“Partner,” he repeated.\n\n“Is that wrong?”\n\n“No.” He looked down at the pavement. “It’s just bigger than girlfriend or boyfriend.”\n\n“We’re bigger than a label.”\n\n“That’s a very romantic thing to say.”\n\n“I’m a romantic.”\n\n“You learned from me.”\n\n“I’m improving on the source material.”\n\nHe laughed, but the worry returned.\n\n“I want to help,” I said. “Not because I’m obligated. Because I choose you.”\n\n“You could use the money for your studio.”\n\n“I can do both.”\n\n“What if this is the start of you carrying me?”\n\n“Then we talk before it becomes that.”\n\nGreg looked at me for a long moment. “You make partnership sound like a conversation instead of a trap.”\n\n“It is a conversation.”\n\n“I’m not used to that.”\n\n“Then we can practice.”\n\nI paid for half the repair. Greg argued. I argued better. The mechanic agreed that my argument had structural integrity and accepted the money.\n\nWhile the van was in the shop, Greg stayed with me in the studio. The room felt smaller with both of us inside it, but not in a bad way. He cooked pasta in the kitchenette. I sat on the counter and read the instructions aloud even though he did not need them.\n\n“The sauce says simmer,” I said.\n\n“I know what simmer means.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\n“I have met a pot before.”\n\n“Your van would disagree.”\n\nHe stirred the sauce. I watched the line of his shoulders, the way he moved around the kitchen without assuming which cabinets belonged to him.\n\n“What?” he asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You’re doing the face.”\n\n“I’m thinking.”\n\n“Dangerous.”\n\n“How do you know when a place is yours?”\n\nGreg turned off the burner.\n\n“That’s a big question for pasta.”\n\n“The pasta can handle it.”\n\nHe leaned against the counter. “Maybe when you stop measuring how much of yourself you’re allowed to leave there.”\n\nI looked around the studio. My books rested on the shelf. My clothes hung in the closet. Greg's guitar pick sat in the dish beside my keys.\n\n“I’m still measuring,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I don’t want to.”\n\n“Then leave something.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Anything.”\n\nI considered the room. Then I took the small framed photograph from my bag. It showed a place I had once called home, before the word became complicated.\n\nI placed it on the shelf beside the records.\n\nGreg did not ask who was in it.\n\n“That’s important,” he said.\n\n“It’s a memory.”\n\n“Those can be important.”\n\nI stepped closer. “What did you leave in the van?”\n\n“Several scarves, a pick, two books, and my heart.”\n\n“Your heart is not an object.”\n\n“It is when I’m writing a song.”\n\n“What song?”\n\nHe touched my photograph, then looked at me. “The one about staying.”\n\nThe kiss began softly. Greg's hand found my waist. I leaned into him, and the kitchen filled with the scent of tomato and basil.\n\n“The pasta,” I whispered.\n\n“It has had a good life.”\n\n“Greg.”\n\nHe turned off the lights. “I’ll save it.”\n\nThe door closed between the kitchen and the rest of the world. Dinner waited. The van waited. The future waited.\n\nFor a while, I did not.\n\nThe next afternoon, the van returned with a new belt, a repaired hose, and a receipt that made Greg physically ill.\n\nHe started the engine. It purred.\n\n“It lives,” he said.\n\n“It has chosen a temporary reprieve.”\n\n“You’re learning.”\n\nHe drove to the beach. I sat beside him, my hand in his.\n\n“Thank you,” he said.\n\n“For paying for the van?”\n\n“For arguing with me.”\n\n“That’s free.”\n\n“It’s not.”\n\nHe parked near the boardwalk and turned to me.\n\n“I don’t want to be saved,” he said. “But I do want to be helped.”\n\nI nodded. “Those are different.”\n\n“I’m learning.”\n\n“We both are.”\n\nGreg kissed my hand. “Partner?”\n\n“Partner.”\n\nThe van carried me home, one repaired part at a time.\n\nThe next week, the mechanic called to say the replacement part had failed.\n\nGreg stared at the phone. “It has betrayed me.”\n\n“It was compatible with optimism,” I reminded him.\n\n“You are enjoying this.”\n\n“A little.”\n\nHe sat on the edge of the new couch in my apartment. The van was parked outside, silent and expensive. the two of us had spent the afternoon listing possible repairs, costs, and alternatives.\n\n“We could sell it,” Greg said.\n\n“You could.”\n\n“I don’t want to.”\n\n“Then repair it.”\n\n“What if repairing it is irresponsible?”\n\n“Then decide what the van is worth to you.”\n\n“You make it sound easy.”\n\n“It’s not easy. It’s just a choice.”\n\nGreg looked down at his hands. “I’m afraid that if I let it go, I’ll lose the last proof that I am still the person who drove away.”\n\n“Do you want to be that person?”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because he did not need anyone.”\n\nThe answer was so sad that I moved closer.\n\n“He also had nowhere to come back to,” I said.\n\nGreg's eyes lifted.\n\n“You don’t have to keep the van to prove you survived,” I continued. “You can keep it because you love it. Or let it go because you are allowed to become someone new.”\n\nHe leaned into my shoulder.\n\n“I hate when you are right,” he murmured.\n\n“I am very difficult to argue with.”\n\n“You are very easy to love.”\n\nThe words softened the room.\n\nThe van was repaired again. Not because it was the only home Greg had, but because it was part of a life he still wanted. He painted the repaired shelf blue. He added my name to a small list of emergency contacts. He put a second blanket in the back.\n\n“For lumbar support?” I asked.\n\n“For emotional lumbar support.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\nI took him by the hand and kissed him in the open van door.\n\nThe city moved around me. The road remained available. The difference was that Greg no longer needed to run every time he felt afraid.\n\nNeither did I.\n\nAt the car wash, Greg played a new song about repairs. The chorus compared a broken belt to a wounded heart. Mara threw a towel at him.\n\n“Too obvious,” she said.\n\n“People like obvious.”\n\n“People like working brakes.”\n\nI laughed from the front row. Greg looked at me while he sang the final verse.\n\nAfterward, he asked whether the song was terrible.\n\n“It is sincere.”\n\n“That is not an answer.”\n\n“It is a kind answer.”\n\n“I’ll take it.”\n\nHe kissed me beside the soap dispensers.\n\n“That was a terrible song,” Mara called.\n\n“You listened to the whole thing,” Greg replied.\n\n“I was trapped by the chorus.”\n\nI smiled at him. “She is jealous of the bridge.”\n\n“Everyone is jealous of the bridge.”\n\n“The bridge is innocent.”\n\n“The bridge knows what it did.”\n\nHe took my hand as the car wash lights came on. The van waited at the curb, repaired and stubborn. We walked home together instead.\n\nThe road could wait until tomorrow.\n\nAt home, Greg wrote the repair date on the calendar. I circled it twice and added a note: BRING SNACKS.\n\nHe added: BRING MORE SNACKS.\n\nThe plan was sound.\n\nEspecially the snacks.\n\nGreg circled the date in blue ink. I added a small star beside it. Neither of us pretended the appointment was more important than the relationship, but both of us intended to keep it.\n\nThe calendar was enough.\n\nI kept the appointment.","word_count":1783},"su-42":{"number":42,"title":"Folding Chairs and Honest Labor","content":"Greg had agreed to perform at a fundraiser before anyone told him there was no one assigned to put away the chairs.\n\nThat was how I found him at Starwake Pier, holding a box of tangled lantern cords in one hand and a folding table under his other arm.\n\n\"How's the glamorous life of a musician?\" I asked.\n\n\"Nobody warned me about furniture.\"\n\n\"Did you read the volunteer sheet?\"\n\n\"Yep.\"\n\n\"Did you put your own name next to every empty task?\"\n\nGreg glanced at the paper pinned to a post. Nearly every line had GREG written beside it.\n\n\"That is a wildly unfair accusation,\" he said. \"Some of those say 'Greg U.'\"\n\nHe was trying to make me laugh, which was ordinarily a successful strategy. I took the table from him instead.\n\n\"You're performing tonight. You also have sound checks, and you're supposed to make sure the speakers don't blow the fuse again. You can't do everything.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You don't sound convinced.\"\n\nGreg set the cords down.\n\n\"People need the money. The pier needs new supports. If somebody says they'll do a job and then doesn't, I can at least make sure it happens.\"\n\n\"I know why you do it. That doesn't make the plan workable.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. The sun caught on the edges of his hair.\n\n\"Do you have a better plan?\"\n\n\"I was hoping we could make one together.\"\n\nWe began with the chairs. There were forty-eight of them, three with loose hinges and one whose left leg did not quite touch the ground. I wrote the number at the top of the volunteer sheet and set the broken ones aside.\n\nGreg inspected the crooked chair.\n\n\"Hey, this one's got character.\"\n\n\"This one has a lawsuit.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThe fundraiser was meant to pay for safer pier railings and a storage shed so equipment wouldn't sit exposed to saltwater all winter. Greg had offered music. Mara had offered food. Tamsin had offered to oversee the repairs. Somewhere between the promises, nobody had agreed who would collect donations or clean up afterward.\n\nI borrowed a pencil from Pip and crossed GREG off the tasks he hadn't been asked to own.\n\n\"Somebody still has to do those,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes. Somebody can volunteer.\"\n\n\"What if they don't?\"\n\n\"Then we scale down the event. We don't pretend you owe free labor to everyone because you know how to play a guitar.\"\n\nHe sat on the edge of the stage. His expression softened, then tightened.\n\n\"Sometimes it feels like if I say no, the whole thing falls apart.\"\n\n\"Then the thing was badly designed.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That's a weirdly comforting insult to an event.\"\n\n\"One of my specialties.\"\n\nWe spent an hour asking people what they could actually do. Katara said her clinic volunteers could help with refreshments for an hour, not all night. Danse agreed to check the temporary stage railings but would not sign off on the damaged western section. Iroh could supervise tea and cleanup until dusk; after that, he had an appointment of his own.\n\nNola arrived for the sound check carrying a case of cables and a schedule that looked significantly less forgiving than Greg's hopeful volunteer sheet.\n\n\"Which of these jobs is paid?\" she asked.\n\nGreg read the paper. \"Uh. None of them?\"\n\n\"Then my crew's only doing the work in the contract. We have another booking tomorrow morning.\"\n\nShe wasn't being cruel. She was watching two sound technicians haul expensive equipment across a damp pier while half the volunteers argued about a banner.\n\nGreg scratched his beard. \"I was going to do the teardown myself.\"\n\n\"You can't disconnect a stage you're still playing on,\" Nola said.\n\n\"Well, when you put it that way.\"\n\nMaui appeared carrying the marked stack of chairs. \"Fear not! The great Maui can clear the entire pier with one hand.\"\n\n\"Please use both,\" Tamsin called from the railing. \"And don't stack them in the exit.\"\n\nMaui looked as though somebody had insulted the artistry of his shoulders. Greg grinned and tried a little drumroll on a chair seat. The chair squealed.\n\n\"Don't give him percussion,\" I said.\n\nNola checked the booking sheet. She moved the sound test earlier and made a separate line for the load-out crew. Danse could check the approved stage railings, but the electrical load would still have to be signed off by the responsible technician.\n\nGreg listened to the new timing. \"So two sets, no mystery third set, and I don't become a chair company afterward.\"\n\n\"Correct,\" Nola said.\n\nHe hummed a rising four-note phrase. It sounded like relief.\n\nMaui stopped beside us. \"Where's my name on the program?\"\n\n\"Under chairs,\" I told him.\n\nHe sighed. \"You people refuse to recognize an artist.\"\n\nMaui offered to carry every chair personally.\n\nI handed him the stack labeled FIFTEEN CHAIRS, TWO PEOPLE.\n\nHe read it.\n\n\"You're limiting my heroism.\"\n\n\"I'm improving its scheduling.\"\n\nAt noon we had six volunteers, a safe seating plan, and a stage that did not require anyone to stand on the broken section. Greg looked at the sheet, then at me.\n\n\"You know the terrible thing?\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"It works.\"\n\n\"I do hate it when that happens.\"\n\nThe first guests arrived while Greg tuned his guitar. Some came from Beach City; others wandered over from the Lanternwell Square market. A child asked whether all the songs would be about doors. Greg promised at least one about chairs, which earned me a look from Mara that could have curdled milk.\n\nA man wearing a wide brown hat stopped beside the stage.\n\n\"You're Greg Universe, right?\"\n\n\"That's me.\"\n\n\"Great! Heard you were playing all night. Can you do another three songs after the fundraiser? My kid's a huge fan.\"\n\nGreg smiled out of reflex.\n\n\"Uh, maybe—\"\n\nI watched the word forming.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\n\"Actually,\" he said, \"I've agreed to two sets. I can sign the kid's program afterward if they want, but I've got to help with the equipment before heading home.\"\n\nThe man blinked.\n\n\"You're not doing an encore?\"\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\nFor a terrible second, I thought the man might argue. Instead, he shrugged and went to find seats.\n\nGreg turned toward me, looking almost surprised.\n\n\"That was uncomfortable.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And the sky did not fall.\"\n\n\"Good. We're running low on replacement sky.\"\n\nHe laughed, then reached for my hand. His fingers were rough against my palm from the guitar strings.\n\n\"You okay with me learning this slowly?\" he asked.\n\n\"I'd be suspicious if you learned it all at once.\"\n\nThe concert went well enough to feel like a real concert instead of a miracle. One microphone buzzed. The first song began a little late because a child dropped a cup in the front row. The audience sang the chorus of Greg's old road song louder than he did.\n\nHalfway through the second set, a light on the pier flickered.\n\nDanse stood at once.\n\nGreg stopped playing.\n\n\"Problem?\"\n\n\"Power fluctuation. Keep everyone off the western platform.\"\n\nThere was no panic. We had actually rehearsed what to do.\n\nMaui helped move chairs to the safer end. Katara brought the children to the square. Greg put down his guitar and checked the speaker plugs while Danse tested the line. They found a loose connection and decided not to restart the stage circuit.\n\nThe remaining songs would be acoustic.\n\n\"I can do acoustic,\" Greg announced to the audience. \"It's mostly what I do anyway.\"\n\nSomeone cheered.\n\nThe night finished with a small circle of people and no dramatic lighting. I preferred it. Greg's voice carried across the water, catching in the warm space between the lanterns. I could see how tired he was, and how happy he was that he hadn't spent the whole evening trying to be four people at once.\n\nWhen the final note faded, he looked at me over the strings.\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe smiled back.\n\nThen he stood up and helped fold the first chair.\n\n\"You're not allowed to do all of them,\" I reminded him.\n\n\"Just this one.\"\n\n\"Fine.\"\n\nWe stacked them in pairs, carried equipment to the dry storeroom, and counted the donation box with Mara present. The fundraiser would cover the railings, not the new shed. That was less than we'd hoped. It was enough to begin.\n\nThe last guest left with a paper cup of tea and the promise that the next event would have a proper cleanup roster.\n\nGreg and I walked toward the Starwake Door after midnight.\n\nHe was carrying only his guitar. I was carrying the repaired volunteer sheet, folded small enough to fit in my pocket.\n\n\"Good night?\" I asked.\n\n\"Best one I've had in a while.\"\n\n\"Even without an encore?\"\n\n\"Especially without one.\"\n\nHe tucked a loose strand of hair behind my ear. The touch was gentle, a question rather than a claim.\n\n\"Can I give you one private encore?\"\n\n\"Depends. Is it about chairs?\"\n\n\"No promises.\"\n\nI kissed him before he could invent a second verse.\n\nLater, when the van door closed behind us, we left the music and the rest of the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning, Greg found the volunteer sheet on the dashboard. Under my handwriting he'd added a new rule:\n\nGREG IS ALLOWED TO BE A MUSICIAN, NOT A FURNITURE COMPANY.\n\nI added one more line beneath it.\n\nJASPER IS NOT THE ENTIRE MANAGEMENT DEPARTMENT EITHER.\n\nHe kissed my cheek while I wrote.\n\nWe both liked the amended version.","word_count":1574}},"after-always":{"after-always-23":{"number":23,"title":"Occlumency","content":"My first lesson in Occlumency began with Snape telling me not to trust him.\n\n“Comforting,” I said.\n\n“Necessary.”\n\nWe were in the research laboratory after hours. The doors were locked. The fireplace was out. No potion simmered between us to provide an excuse for attention.\n\nSnape stood across the room with his wand lowered.\n\n“If I enter your mind, your instinct will be to follow whatever image rises,” he said. “Do not. Observe the intrusion. Do not assist it.”\n\n“You make thoughts sound like unhelpful houseguests.”\n\n“Yours are.”\n\nI ignored that.\n\n“Can you see everything?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Can you choose what you see?”\n\n“To a degree.”\n\n“Can I stop you?”\n\n“That is the point.”\n\n“Can you stop if I tell you to?”\n\nHis expression changed.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Immediately?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then that's the rule.”\n\n“It was already the rule.”\n\n“I need to hear you say it.”\n\nA pause.\n\nSnape nodded.\n\n“If you tell me to stop, I stop.”\n\nOnly then did I raise my wand.\n\nHe noticed the sequence.\n\nOf course he did.\n\n“You do not trust me,” he said.\n\n“I trust you enough to try.”\n\n“That is not the same thing.”\n\n“No.”\n\nSomething like approval crossed his face.\n\n“Good.”\n\nThe first attempt lasted less than a second.\n\n“Legilimens.”\n\nThe room vanished.\n\nA flash of light.\n\nA door.\n\nMy own laughter.\n\nA fear I had not invited.\n\nI shoved mentally at everything at once.\n\nSnape was gone immediately.\n\nI staggered.\n\nHe did not catch me.\n\nRule.\n\nI steadied myself on the table.\n\nSeverus put the wand on the bench between us, well out of my reach but not hidden.\n\n\"You attempted to stop the intrusion by destroying every thought at once,\" he said.\n\n\"I was aiming for efficient.\"\n\n\"You were aiming for chaos. A common mistake in your repertoire.\"\n\nI wanted to snap back. Instead I looked at the scorch mark beside the table leg, left over from a much older experiment. He had chosen a room with a visible exit and no simmering cauldron. Even his caution looked unfriendly.\n\n\"Can we make a better signal?\" I asked. \"Something for when words don't come out.\"\n\nHe considered the suggestion as though it had arrived from an irritatingly competent colleague.\n\n\"Two taps on the table,\" he said. \"An agreed stop signal. Not an indication of failure.\"\n\n\"And if I use it because I'm afraid?\"\n\n\"Then you use it because you are afraid.\"\n\nHe took out a sheet of parchment and wrote the procedure in a narrow, precise hand: one attempt; immediate withdrawal at the signal; no account of any recovered image unless I chose to give one; rest before trying again. He underlined the last rule.\n\n\"You're making yourself a protocol,\" I said.\n\n\"I'm making it possible to find out whether either of us has violated one.\"\n\nThe distinction mattered. It wasn't soothing. It was something I could check.\n\nWhen we tried again, a memory rose like a door opening into bright weather. I tapped twice before I knew what it contained. Severus lowered his wand instantly.\n\n\"Again?\" he asked.\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\n\"Very well.\"\n\nHe looked disappointed, but he wrote *session ended by student* and put the quill away. Nothing about my mind was added to the page.\n\n“Terrible,” he said.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You attempted to suppress the memories.”\n\n“What else was I supposed to do?”\n\n“Detach.”\n\n“That is not an instruction.”\n\n“It is the only instruction that matters.”\n\nI glared.\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“Again.”\n\nThe second attempt lasted two seconds.\n\nThe third, four.\n\nBy the fifth, I learned the difference between hiding a thought and refusing to chase it.\n\nSnape entered.\n\nAn image rose.\n\nI let it pass.\n\nAnother.\n\nI let it pass.\n\nThen his own face appeared in my mind.\n\nNot from the present.\n\nHogsmeade rain.\n\nHis little finger hooking around mine beneath the umbrella.\n\nMy concentration shattered.\n\nSnape broke the spell instantly.\n\nWe stared at each other.\n\nHe had seen it.\n\nI knew he had.\n\nColor rose beneath his sallow skin.\n\n“Well,” I said.\n\nHis voice was extremely controlled. “Your defenses remain inadequate.”\n\n“That's what you took from that?”\n\n“You followed the memory.”\n\n“You were in it.”\n\n“Which is irrelevant.”\n\n“It was relevant to me.”\n\nHis eyes flashed.\n\nThe tension shifted.\n\nNot embarrassment now.\n\nAwareness.\n\n“Again,” he said.\n\nI lifted my wand.\n\n“Are you sure?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You may see worse.”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“You may see better.”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“I am aware.”\n\nI smiled faintly.\n\n“Stop enjoying this.”\n\n“I am not.”\n\n“Liar.”\n\n“Close your mind.”\n\nHe cast.\n\nThis time I was ready for him.\n\nMemories rose.\n\nI did not chase them.\n\nA room.\n\nA voice.\n\nA hand in mine.\n\nThe first time I saw Snape smile.\n\nThe thought tried to pull me toward it.\n\nI let it float past.\n\nThen something darker rose.\n\nA private grief.\n\nI felt Snape retreat before I asked.\n\nThe spell ended.\n\nI opened my eyes.\n\nHe had moved half a step backward.\n\n“Why did you stop?”\n\n“Your concentration broke.”\n\n“No. It didn't.”\n\nHis face hardened.\n\n“You saw something you decided not to enter.”\n\n“I told you I can choose to a degree.”\n\n“That isn't what I asked.”\n\nHe looked away.\n\nI understood.\n\nHe had found a boundary I had not named and treated it as real anyway.\n\nTrust shifted again.\n\nSmall.\n\nPrecise.\n\nLike an ingredient added at the correct second.\n\n“Thank you,” I said.\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“Do not thank me for basic ethics.”\n\n“Plenty of people fail them.”\n\n“That does not make success exceptional.”\n\n“Fine. I won't praise you.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“You were decent.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“Worse.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nWe took a break.\n\nSnape brewed tea while I sat on the edge of a table and tried to settle my thoughts.\n\n“Is this how you hid from Voldemort?” I asked.\n\nHe paused.\n\n“In part.”\n\n“What did you do with the memories you couldn't afford him seeing?”\n\n“Contained them.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“Practice.”\n\n“That is annoyingly vague.”\n\n“It is also true.”\n\nHe handed me tea.\n\nI took it.\n\n“Were you afraid every time he looked at you?”\n\nSnape's eyes went still.\n\nI almost withdrew the question.\n\nThen he said, “Yes.”\n\nThe honesty landed hard.\n\n“He was better at Legilimency than I was.”\n\n“Better?”\n\n“More powerful. Less restrained.”\n\n“How did you keep him out?”\n\n“I did not keep him out.”\n\nI stared.\n\nSnape sat opposite me.\n\n“That is the mistake most people make when imagining Occlumency. A perfect wall is suspicious. I allowed him access to what I wanted him to find.”\n\nThe implication made my skin cold.\n\n“You built lies out of real memories.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You let him see enough truth to believe the rest.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For years.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI looked at him differently then.\n\nNot admiringly.\n\nWith horror at the endurance it required.\n\n“Did you know who you were after a while?”\n\nHis eyes snapped to mine.\n\nThe question had escaped unplanned.\n\nI expected anger.\n\nInstead he looked tired.\n\n“Sometimes,” he said.\n\nThe word sat between us.\n\nI set down the cup.\n\n“Can I touch you?”\n\nHis gaze sharpened, then softened by a fraction.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI held out my hand.\n\nHe took it.\n\nNo drama.\n\nNo mark exposed.\n\nJust fingers closing around mine.\n\n“I know who you are now,” I said.\n\nHis face changed.\n\n“That is an extraordinary claim.”\n\n“I didn't say I know everything.”\n\n“Then clarify.”\n\n“I know who you are when you're with me.”\n\nHis thumb moved over my knuckle.\n\n“And who is that?”\n\n“A man who drinks terrible tea, weaponizes vocabulary, cheats at emotional conversations, and bought me very good gloves.”\n\nHis mouth curved.\n\n“Trivial.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nThe smile faded into something softer.\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\n“Not a spy. Not a Death Eater. Not a martyr. Not Lily's tragedy. Just you, for an hour.”\n\nSnape looked at our hands.\n\n“That sounds dangerous.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\nHe did not let go.\n\nWhen we resumed Occlumency, I lasted almost ten seconds.\n\nThe final memory that rose was Snape's hand in mine.\n\nThis time I let it pass without following.\n\nWhen the spell ended, he looked almost proud.\n\n“Better,” he said.\n\nI smiled.\n\nThe next lesson, Severus put a bowl of water on the desk between us.\n\nI looked at it. “Are we hydrating?”\n\n“We are establishing a recovery cue.”\n\nThat sounded suspiciously healthy.\n\nHe ignored my expression.\n\n“If you become disoriented after contact, you will place both hands around the bowl and name five observable properties.”\n\n“Cold, wet, round—”\n\n“During the exercise.”\n\n“You're no fun.”\n\n“I am teaching you to prevent magical intrusion from becoming panic.”\n\n“Yes, Professor.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\nThe exercise went badly twice and well once. On the fourth attempt, I lost track of the room entirely and came out gasping.\n\nSeverus did not reach for me.\n\n“Bowl,” he said.\n\nI found it.\n\n“Five things.”\n\n“Cold. Ceramic. Blue. Chipped at the rim. Heavier than it looks.”\n\n“Four.”\n\n“That's five.”\n\n“Heavier is an inference.”\n\nI glared at him.\n\nThe room snapped fully back into focus.\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“You did that on purpose.”\n\n“Anger is grounding.”\n\n“That is the most Severus Snape therapeutic technique imaginable.”\n\n“I am not your therapist.”\n\n“Thank Merlin.”\n\nHe almost smiled.\n\nMore importantly, he had brought me back without taking control away from me.\n\nThis time he allowed it.","word_count":1528}},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-37":{"number":37,"title":"The Name He Chose","content":"I asked Danse whether he wanted another name.\n\nHe nearly dropped a wrench.\n\n“What?”\n\nWe were repairing the settlement generator. He had one arm inside the housing and grease across his knuckles.\n\n“Danse. If that identity was built on memories you don’t trust, do you want something else?”\n\nHis expression became guarded.\n\n“I hadn’t considered it.”\n\n“You don’t have to.”\n\nHe returned to the generator.\n\nFor the rest of the afternoon he said almost nothing.\n\nThe generator still needed an operating label. A settlement worker brought us a sheet with blank boxes for inspection, handover and responsibility.\n\n\"Should I put Paladin Danse?\" she asked.\n\nHis wrench stopped moving.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Right. Sorry. Just Danse, then?\"\n\nHe looked at the sheet. There were no Brotherhood insignia on the tool bench, no officer to authorize the answer. Only a valve that could fail if somebody hurried the final test.\n\n\"Put Danse for the repair,\" he said. \"Put your name under operation. You run the generator after I leave.\"\n\nShe squinted at the wiring diagram. \"I wasn't there when you replaced that part.\"\n\n\"Then you shouldn't sign the inspection until I show you the measurements.\"\n\nHe walked her through the load test. His tone was clipped and occasionally too sharp. Twice she asked him to slow down. Both times he did, though his jaw tightened as though patience were a muscle he hadn't exercised in years.\n\nShe wrote the numbers herself and asked what to do if the current rose again.\n\n\"Isolate the circuit. Call for assistance. Do not attempt an improvised bypass.\"\n\n\"That's a lot of orders for a man who just told me I'm in charge.\"\n\nHe considered that. \"It's a procedure. You can challenge the procedure.\"\n\nShe did. One of his steps assumed the panel could be reached from the right. The worker demonstrated why that wasn't always possible, and he amended the diagram.\n\nI watched him write his name beneath the repair instead of a rank. The letters looked ordinary. He checked the torque on the last bolt and went back to work, as though being ordinary required extraordinary concentration.\n\nI assumed I had pushed too far.\n\nThat evening he disappeared.\n\nNot dramatically. His rifle was gone from the rack, which meant patrol. Still, after dark I started to worry.\n\nHe returned near midnight carrying a small metal sign.\n\nDANSE.\n\nThe letters were faded, salvaged from some pre-War studio or school.\n\nI stared.\n\n“Where did you find that?”\n\n“Old dance academy in Cambridge.”\n\nI laughed before I could stop myself.\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n“You stole your name from a dance studio?”\n\n“I did not steal it. I recovered signage.”\n\n“This is incredible.”\n\n“You asked a serious question.”\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\nI was not sorry.\n\nDanse set the sign on the table.\n\n“I thought about choosing something new.”\n\nMy laughter faded.\n\n“And?”\n\n“I don’t want to.”\n\nHe touched the rusted edge of the sign.\n\n“Whether someone gave me the name or I picked it from a memory that wasn’t mine, I’ve answered to it for years.”\n\n“That makes it yours.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“I made decisions as Danse. Good and bad. People trusted Danse. People died under Danse. You…”\n\nHe stopped.\n\n“I what?”\n\n“You love Danse.”\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis expression softened.\n\n“Then I think I’ll keep it.”\n\nI crossed the room and wrapped my arms around him.\n\nHe held me.\n\nAfter a minute I said into his shirt, “We are absolutely hanging that sign over the workshop.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Danse’s Dance and Laser Repair.”\n\nHe pulled back in horror.\n\n“I will burn it.”\n\n“You carried it six miles.”\n\n“I regret everything.”\n\nI laughed so hard I had to sit down.\n\nThe next morning the sign was mounted over the workbench.\n\nNot because I did it.\n\nDanse did.\n\nHe pretended it was a practical label.\n\nNobody believed him.\n\nA week later, a trader stopped by with a broken laser pistol.\n\n“Danse?” she asked, looking at the sign.\n\nHe looked up from the bench.\n\n“That’s me.”\n\nNo rank.\n\nNo registration number.\n\nNo explanation.\n\nThat’s me.\n\nI turned away before he saw me smile.\n\nThe sign created a second problem.\n\nPeople started calling the workshop Danse’s.\n\nNot *the repair bench.*\n\nNot *that place near the eastern settlement.*\n\nDanse’s.\n\nThe first time I heard it was from a caravan guard who stopped beside the road and asked, “This Danse’s repair shop?”\n\nDanse looked up from a laser musket.\n\n“It is not a shop.”\n\nThe guard pointed at the sign.\n\nDANSE.\n\nThen at the workbench.\n\nThen at the three rifles waiting for repair.\n\n“What would you call it?”\n\nDanse opened his mouth.\n\nClosed it.\n\nI saved him.\n\n“A deeply confusing hobby.”\n\nThe guard nodded as if that made sense.\n\nTwo weeks later we had a shelf for incoming work.\n\nOne week after that Danse made numbered tags.\n\nI found him writing a ledger at the kitchen table.\n\n“You have opened a business.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You have inventory numbers.”\n\n“Those prevent confusion.”\n\n“You charge people.”\n\n“Only enough to cover parts.”\n\n“You have business hours.”\n\n“I have times when I prefer not to be interrupted.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe put down the pencil.\n\n“Fine. It may have some characteristics of a business.”\n\nI kissed the top of his head.\n\n“Proud of you.”\n\n“That is unnecessary.”\n\n“Still true.”\n\nThe stranger thing was how quickly his name became detached from the Brotherhood.\n\nA child came by one morning and shouted, “Danse! My mom says the pump’s making the noise again!”\n\nDanse grabbed his tool bag and went.\n\nNo one said Paladin.\n\nNo one asked for identification.\n\nHis name meant the man who could fix the pump.\n\nThe man with the red scarf.\n\nThe man who would explain why a rifle jammed instead of simply taking it away.\n\nThe man whose coffee should be refused under all circumstances.\n\nA name built new associations every time someone used it.\n\nThat mattered more than I expected.\n\nOne evening, after the workshop closed, Danse and I sat beneath the sign cleaning tools.\n\n“Do you remember why you chose ‘Danse’ originally?” I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n“No. If the memory was implanted, I may never know.”\n\n“Does that bother you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHonest as ever now.\n\n“But less.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nHe looked up at the letters.\n\n“Because I know why I’m choosing it today.”\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\nHe continued before I could turn the moment into something unbearable.\n\n“Also, changing it would create unnecessary paperwork.”\n\n“There he is.”\n\nDanse smiled.\n\nA few days later Lena appeared on the road.\n\nThe escaped synth from the culvert carried a backpack and looked exhausted but alive.\n\nDanse was outside splitting wood.\n\nHe froze when he saw her.\n\nShe stopped several feet away.\n\n“You said to ask for Mara.”\n\n“I did.”\n\n“She helped me.”\n\nDanse set down the axe.\n\n“I’m glad.”\n\nLena glanced at the sign over the workshop.\n\n“So you’re Danse.”\n\nThe statement hit him differently than every other use of the name.\n\nNot recognition from a soldier.\n\nNot affection from me.\n\nA synth looking at another synth who had chosen to live under the name he already had.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nLena smiled weakly.\n\n“I picked mine from a book.”\n\nDanse’s eyebrows rose.\n\n“Was it a good book?”\n\n“No.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nLena did too.\n\nDanse looked between us with the long-suffering expression of a man realizing he had been outnumbered by terrible literary taste.\n\nWe invited her to dinner.\n\nShe told us she had found temporary work repairing clothing. She had nightmares about the Institute. She hated carrots. She liked radio dramas. She did not know whether any of those preferences had been planted in her.\n\nDanse listened.\n\nReally listened.\n\nAt one point Lena said, “Sometimes I think I should choose a whole new identity. New name. New everything. Like if I keep anything they gave me, they still own it.”\n\nDanse looked at the rusted sign visible through the window.\n\n“They don’t.”\n\nLena’s eyes lifted.\n\n“You sure?”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n“But I’m learning.”\n\nLena’s question stayed with him after the workshop closed.\n\nThat night he opened the storage trunk and took out the old Brotherhood undersuit.\n\nThe registration code was still stamped inside the collar: DN-407P.\n\n“Danse,” I said quietly.\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe ran his thumb over the letters.\n\n“DN. The Brotherhood derived it from my name.”\n\n“So?”\n\n“So I used to think the registration proved the name belonged there.”\n\nHe folded the collar back down.\n\n“Now it feels the other way around.”\n\n“The code belonged to you because the name did.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe sat on the edge of the bed.\n\n“Paladin Krieg sponsored my training. Maxson promoted me. The Brotherhood gave me ranks, duties, a place to stand.”\n\n“But not the name?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Who did?”\n\nDanse frowned.\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\nThe Institute, perhaps. The Railroad, perhaps. Some memory technician whose face he would never see.\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n“You could still change it.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Do you want to?”\n\nHe considered longer than before.\n\n“No.”\n\nThis time the answer was certain.\n\n“I want this one.”\n\nThe workshop sign had been his idea, though he denied this whenever I mentioned it.\n\nOriginally it had said REPAIRS.\n\nA child from the settlement painted DANSE above it because, according to her, “people need to know whose repairs.”\n\nDanse had stared at the wet letters for almost a minute.\n\n“You can repaint it,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Too crooked?”\n\n“Very.”\n\n“Wrong color?”\n\n“Also yes.”\n\n“But?”\n\nHe looked at the child, who was waiting for judgment with paint on both cheeks.\n\n“It’s legible.”\n\nThe child beamed.\n\nDanse left the crooked name exactly where it was.\n\nWeeks later, everyone used it as directions.\n\nGo past Danse’s shop. Ask Danse about the pump. Danse has the right wrench.\n\nA name could become a place people expected to find him.\n\nThe letters were still crooked. Danse had repaired the roof twice, replaced the hinge, leveled the bench, and reorganized every shelf.\n\nHe never repainted his name.\n\nHe traced one crooked letter with a grease-stained thumb, then left the paint exactly as it was.\n\nAfter she left, I found him standing beneath the sign.\n\n“That was good,” I said.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You didn’t turn yourself into a lesson.”\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“I don’t know what that means.”\n\n“You told her your answer without pretending it had to be hers.”\n\nDanse considered.\n\n“People are not exceptions to rules.”\n\n“No?”\n\n“They’re… individual cases.”\n\n“That is the most bureaucratic path possible to compassion.”\n\n“I’m working with available skills.”\n\nI wrapped my arms around him.\n\nHe held me against his chest.\n\nAbove us, the sign creaked in the evening wind.\n\nDANSE.\n\nNot the name the Institute owned.\n\nNot the name the Brotherhood revoked.\n\nThe name he kept answering to because every day he chose to.\n","word_count":1784}},"love-and-duty":{"Love-And-Duty-24":{"number":24,"title":"Aang’s Terrible, Wonderful Idea","content":"Aang’s terrible idea began with the sentence, “Technically, I checked the rules.”\n\nI put down my tea.\n\nKatara closed her eyes.\n\nToph grinned.\n\n“No,” Katara said.\n\n“You don’t know what it is.”\n\n“I know you.”\n\n“That’s unfair.”\n\n“Is it?”\n\nAang stood in our kitchen wearing the expression he had whenever being the Avatar temporarily became less important than being the same person who once invented an air scooter because walking was boring.\n\nHe held four paper tickets.\n\nToph tilted her head. “Race?”\n\nAang looked betrayed. “How did you know?”\n\n“You’re vibrating.”\n\n“I am not.”\n\n“You are emotionally vibrating.”\n\nI took one ticket from his hand.\n\nWINTER FOUNDERS’ FESTIVAL — CHARITY STREET COURSE.\n\nBelow it, in smaller print:\n\nNON-MOTORIZED VEHICLES ONLY. BENDING-PROPULSION PERMITTED IN OPEN CLASS.\n\nI looked at Aang.\n\n“No.”\n\nHis face fell.\n\n“I haven’t explained.”\n\n“You want us to enter.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“In what vehicle?”\n\nHis grin returned.\n\nThat was the second warning.\n\nThe vehicle was in the courtyard.\n\nCalling it a vehicle was generous.\n\nIt looked like a tea cart had fallen in love with a glider and produced offspring during a storm.\n\nFour wheels. Wooden frame. Cloth wing. Steering bar. Two seats in front, two in back. A metal plate ran beneath the chassis for Toph to bend against if necessary. A small water reservoir sat behind Katara’s seat.\n\nI stared.\n\nKatara stared.\n\nToph walked around it once, feeling the metal through her feet.\n\n“This is the stupidest thing you’ve ever built.”\n\nAang beamed. “Thank you.”\n\n“I wasn’t complimenting you.”\n\n“I’m keeping it.”\n\nI circled the machine.\n\n“Who designed the brakes?”\n\nAang pointed at Sokka’s signature scratched into the rear axle.\n\nI relaxed slightly.\n\nThen I noticed a second note underneath.\n\nTOPH CHANGED THEM. GOOD LUCK.\n\nI stopped relaxing.\n\nThe race was two days later.\n\nOn the first trial run, Aang insisted the machine only needed a little more lift.\n\n\"How much is a little?\" Katara asked.\n\n\"Enough to make it fun.\"\n\nToph stomped once beside the axle. \"The left brake is loose.\"\n\nAang looked down. \"I checked it.\"\n\n\"You checked whether it existed. Different question, Twinkle Toes.\"\n\nI wanted to laugh, but a delivery cart was coming across the testing yard. Aang's gust lifted our front wheel an inch before Katara seized the frame and demanded he stop. The cart driver had to pull back hard to avoid us.\n\nHis name was Beren, and he wasn't interested in whether the Avatar had meant well. He pointed to the rut his cart had left.\n\n\"That's where my load goes every morning. If your race blocks the route, someone else misses their delivery.\"\n\nAang's face fell. \"I didn't realize this was the only usable way through.\"\n\n\"Then ask before you decide it isn't.\"\n\nToph crouched, laid one hand against the axle and found the loose connection. She wouldn't certify the fix until Sokka checked the drawing. Katara walked the planned path with Beren to see where a temporary crossing could actually be made. I measured the width and wrote down what he said instead of guessing.\n\nAang helped move the testing frame out of the way.\n\n\"You could still make it fly,\" I told him as we cleared the yard.\n\n\"I know.\" He looked toward Beren's wagon. \"But then we'd have to land somewhere.\"\n\n\"Congratulations,\" Toph said. \"You invented consequences.\"\n\nAang laughed. He sounded a little embarrassed, which was an improvement over his first plan to take the corner by air.\n\nWe spent those days arguing about roles.\n\nAang wanted to steer.\n\nKatara said Aang’s idea of steering included vertical space.\n\nToph wanted control of the brakes because they were metal.\n\nI wanted to live.\n\nWe compromised.\n\nPoorly.\n\nOn race morning, Cranefish Town was packed with spectators. The route wound from the market district, across the eastern bridge, around the old refinery, and down a long slope to the river square.\n\nCompetitors included messenger carts, sand-sailer adaptations, a pair of Fire Nation engineers with a pedal-driven machine, and one old woman pushing what appeared to be a wheelbarrow with absolute confidence.\n\nToph pointed toward her.\n\n“She’s the threat.”\n\n“You can tell?” I asked.\n\n“She has no fear.”\n\nAang climbed into the front seat.\n\nKatara sat beside him.\n\nToph and I took the rear.\n\n“This is a date,” Aang announced.\n\n“This is attempted murder,” Katara replied.\n\n“Romantic murder.”\n\n“Drive.”\n\nThe starting gong sounded.\n\nEverything became noise.\n\nAang launched us forward with a burst of air.\n\nI screamed.\n\nToph laughed.\n\nKatara shouted, “LEFT!”\n\nAang shouted, “I KNOW!”\n\nWe turned right.\n\nThe Fire Nation pedal machine clipped a stack of hay bales. The wheelbarrow grandmother passed us on the inside.\n\nToph slapped both feet against the floor plate and bent the rear axle just enough to pivot us around a vegetable cart.\n\nA cabbage rolled through the air.\n\nSomewhere, I felt history repeat itself.\n\n“We’re going too fast!” I yelled.\n\nAang looked over his shoulder.\n\n“We’re in seventh!”\n\n“That is not an answer!”\n\nKatara bent a ribbon of water from the reservoir and froze it along one runner as we hit a patch of mud. The cart slid sideways, perfectly controlled for approximately two seconds and then absolutely not controlled at all.\n\nToph whooped.\n\nI discovered new prayers.\n\nWe reached the eastern bridge in fourth place.\n\nAang could have won easily if he stopped pretending the machine mattered and simply airbent us down the route.\n\nHe refused.\n\n“That’s cheating!”\n\n“Bending propulsion is allowed!” Katara yelled.\n\n“Too much is spiritually cheating!”\n\nToph shouted, “Your spirituality is losing!”\n\nThe bridge narrowed.\n\nTwo carts entered ahead of us side by side.\n\nAang pulled back.\n\nI expected frustration.\n\nInstead he laughed.\n\n“We go over.”\n\nKatara’s eyes widened. “Aang—”\n\nAir hit beneath the cloth wing.\n\nThe cart lifted.\n\nNot high.\n\nHigh enough.\n\nWe sailed over the gap between the two competitors and landed with every wheel striking stone at once.\n\nMy teeth met hard enough to see stars.\n\nToph was laughing so violently she could barely bend.\n\nKatara stared at Aang.\n\nHe grinned.\n\nShe grabbed his face and kissed him while he was still steering.\n\n“We are going to die,” I said.\n\nToph slapped my knee. “Worth it!”\n\nThe refinery turn brought us beside the wheelbarrow grandmother.\n\nShe glanced over.\n\n“Too many people!” she shouted.\n\n“You’re terrifying!” Aang shouted back.\n\nShe smiled.\n\nThen passed us again.\n\nWe finished third.\n\nThe grandmother won.\n\nNobody was surprised.\n\nAt the award table, Aang bowed to her.\n\n“What’s your secret?”\n\nShe looked at him as if the Avatar were a somewhat slow child.\n\n“Downhill.”\n\nToph nearly collapsed.\n\nThe prize money went to the city’s winter shelter fund. Our third-place ribbon went to Toph, who claimed she had contributed the most by preventing the rest of us from dying.\n\nKatara argued that she had prevented at least two deaths personally.\n\nI argued that I had served as ballast.\n\nAang argued that joy was its own contribution.\n\nWe threw snow at him.\n\nThat should have been the end of the day.\n\nInstead, Aang dragged us to the festival proper.\n\nWe ate fried dough. Katara won a carved polar bear dog at a ring toss and insisted she had not used waterbending. Toph challenged a strength-test machine and broke it. I spent an irresponsible amount of money on roasted chestnuts.\n\nFor six hours, nobody asked Aang to mediate anything.\n\nNobody asked Katara to heal them.\n\nNobody asked Toph to build, teach, detect lies, or represent metalbenders.\n\nNobody handed me a form.\n\nWe were not symbols.\n\nWe were four idiots sharing one scarf because Aang had lost his, Toph refused to wear one properly, and Katara had decided hypothermia was a collective moral failure.\n\nNear sunset, music began in the square.\n\nAang reached for my hand.\n\nThis time he did not scan the crowd first.\n\nI noticed.\n\nHe noticed me noticing.\n\nHis fingers tightened.\n\n“Dance?”\n\n“I saw how you drove.”\n\n“That’s unrelated.”\n\n“It feels related.”\n\nKatara took my other hand.\n\n“Come on.”\n\nToph said, “I don’t dance.”\n\nThree of us looked at her.\n\nShe sighed.\n\n“I hate this family.”\n\n“You chose us,” Aang said.\n\n“Worst mistake of my life.”\n\nShe joined anyway.\n\nAang moved like air even with his boots on the ground.\n\nKatara followed rhythm the way she followed water, graceful until she laughed too hard and missed a step.\n\nToph danced by feeling everyone else’s feet through the stone and then deliberately doing something different.\n\nI was not good.\n\nNobody cared.\n\nAt one point Aang spun me beneath his arm, Katara caught me when I overbalanced, and Toph complained that our choreography was “aggressively sighted.”\n\nSo we changed it.\n\nToph put one hand on my shoulder, one on Katara’s. Aang kept contact through our linked arms. We stopped relying on visual cues and moved to pressure, rhythm, laughter, the vibration of drums under our feet.\n\nToph smiled.\n\nNot because we had included her.\n\nBecause we had adapted together without turning adaptation into charity.\n\nThat distinction mattered.\n\nNight settled over the square.\n\nLanterns reflected in patches of melted snow.\n\nAang pressed his forehead to mine when the music slowed.\n\n“Terrible idea?”\n\nI looked at Katara, cheeks pink from cold and dancing. Toph had stolen the third-place ribbon and tied it around her wrist like a championship belt.\n\n“Terrible,” I said.\n\nAang smiled.\n\n“Wonderful?”\n\nI kissed him.\n\n“Unfortunately.”\n\nThe terrible idea was an Air Nomad cultural center with a public kitchen, meditation hall, archive room, rooftop garden, and absolutely no clear source of funding.\n\nAang had drawn the first plan himself.\n\nThis was obvious because every corridor curved beautifully and none of the storage rooms were large enough to hold anything larger than a monk’s optimism.\n\nKatara spread the papers over the table. “Where do supplies go?”\n\nAang pointed. “There.”\n\n“That’s a window.”\n\n“It could become shelves.”\n\nToph leaned over the plan, unable to see it and therefore judging it with complete confidence. “Floor’s bad.”\n\n“You haven’t touched the floor. It doesn’t exist yet.”\n\n“I can tell.”\n\nI traced the public entrance. “If this is for teaching Air Nomad culture, who gets to teach?”\n\nAang’s excitement faltered.\n\nThat was the real question beneath all the architecture.\n\n“I do, at first,” he said.\n\n“And when people know parts you don’t?” Katara asked gently.\n\nHe looked at her.\n\nThere were scraps of Air Nomad history scattered across the world: temple records, old songs remembered imperfectly by families, artifacts in private collections, stories altered by a century of retelling. Aang carried more than anyone alive. He did not carry everything.\n\n“I invite them,” he said slowly.\n\nToph tapped the table. “Even if they disagree with you?”\n\nHis mouth twisted.\n\n“Especially then, probably.”\n\n“That sounds awful.”\n\n“It does.”\n\nI smiled. “Still wonderful.”\n\nAang looked at the plans again, this time with less certainty and more possibility.\n\nBy midnight we had added storage, ramps, a community advisory table, a rule against treating every surviving artifact as sacred merely because it was old, and an entire page labeled THINGS AANG DOES NOT HAVE TO KNOW ALONE.\n\nHe circled that heading.\n\nThen he drew a tiny sky bison beside it.\n\nToph felt the fresh ink with one fingertip.\n\n“Terrible drawing,” she said.\n\n“You can’t see it.”\n\n“I can feel the lack of talent.”\n\nWonderful, unfortunately, survived another meeting.\n","word_count":1866},"love-and-duty-43":{"number":43,"title":"A Letter to the South","content":"The letter from the South Pole arrived folded inside a cloth packet stained with travel salt.\n\nKatara recognized the stitching before she read a word.\n\n\"My aunt made this,\" she said.\n\nHer fingers paused over the blue thread along the seam. It was the kind of pause that told me the letter mattered before I knew what it contained.\n\nAang stood by the window with Appa's feed schedule in his hand. Toph had one foot against the floor, listening to the delivery cart pull away.\n\nI stayed at the table and let Katara open it herself.\n\nShe read the first page quickly. On the second, her expression changed.\n\n\"Someone is sick?\" Aang asked.\n\n\"Not exactly.\"\n\nShe finished reading.\n\n\"The southern clinic is running low on certain herbs. The supply ships have been delayed. There are more patients than beds after the ice-breakup storms, and the healers want me to come help organize a new treatment room.\"\n\nAang put down the schedule.\n\n\"When do they need you?\"\n\n\"Now.\"\n\nToph leaned back in her chair.\n\n\"Well, that's a terrible invitation.\"\n\nKatara folded the letter along its old creases.\n\n\"It's home.\"\n\nNobody argued with that.\n\nThe harder part came later.\n\nWe had been planning to attend the Riverstone bridge inspection together, the first one after the repairs. Katara was supposed to lead the clinic volunteers at the reopened crossing. Aang had promised to speak to the council. Toph had already threatened the mortar with consequences.\n\nAnd I had planned a whole evening with Katara.\n\nI looked at the calendar we'd spent so much time making.\n\n\"How soon can you leave?\" I asked.\n\nShe looked at me with an expression I couldn't read.\n\n\"Tomorrow. Perhaps tonight if Appa is ready.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Then we need to find someone who can cover the clinic.\"\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\nI looked up.\n\n\"You're not angry?\"\n\n\"About the letter?\"\n\n\"About leaving.\"\n\n\"Why would I be angry that people you love need you?\"\n\nShe set the letter on the table.\n\n\"Because we've barely finished deciding how to make time for each other. Because I've had to leave before. Because you have other relationships and I know you don't need me here every night.\"\n\nThe words came out in a rush. Then she looked embarrassed by how much she'd revealed.\n\nI went around the table and stood where she could see me.\n\n\"I don't need you here every night to care about you,\" I said. \"I want you to have a home you can leave and return to. I also want you to tell me when you're scared I'll stop waiting. Those are different conversations.\"\n\nToph nodded once.\n\n\"Finally, somebody said it.\"\n\nAang rubbed the back of his neck.\n\n\"I think I've been afraid of that too,\" he admitted.\n\nKatara looked at him.\n\n\"Afraid of what?\"\n\n\"That every time duty takes us in different directions, we come back a little less like we were.\"\n\nToph made a face.\n\n\"Of course we do. That's the point of living.\"\n\nWe all looked at her.\n\nShe lifted her chin.\n\n\"What? You want a relationship with people who never change? Try dating statues.\"\n\nI laughed, startled. Katara's mouth twitched.\n\nToph leaned forward.\n\n\"You'll go South. We'll miss you. You'll come back. We'll talk about what happened, and maybe something will be different. That's not a tragedy. It's just a Wednesday with a lot of snow.\"\n\nKatara crossed the room and hugged her so suddenly that Toph made an outraged noise.\n\n\"Stop making me look emotionally available!\"\n\n\"Too late,\" I said.\n\nAang joined them. I stayed back until Katara reached for me.\n\nWe spent the afternoon planning what needed planning. Aang arranged travel with Appa. I spoke to the clinic volunteers about interim shifts. Toph checked the footbridge one more time so the committee could begin its work without Katara having to carry the entire project from another continent.\n\nThe first proposed supply list came back from the Southern clinic with half its entries crossed out.\n\nKatara stared at it. \"They don't need more blankets. They need clean treatment space, fish oil and the right herbs. Sending the wrong things just fills the room they are trying to repair.\"\n\nAang had already drawn a route for Appa. \"I could carry another load.\"\n\n\"You can carry what the clinic coordinator actually requests,\" Katara said. \"Not whatever makes us feel useful.\"\n\nToph tapped the floor beside the table. \"And don't forget the platform. Their last letter said the steps were iced over.\"\n\n\"I remember,\" Katara said. \"They have a plan to clear them. I want to know if the path is wide enough for the equipment.\"\n\nI checked the list of Lanternweald garden stores. Tideglass had some useful plants, but the coastal beds were not an endless apothecary and not a substitute for medicine selected by healers familiar with the South. I left the quantities blank until the clinic could confirm what it would accept.\n\nAang crossed out his extra cargo run. \"Okay. No heroic surprise shipment.\"\n\n\"You're learning,\" Toph said.\n\nHe gave her a wounded look. \"I thought this was about helping.\"\n\n\"It is,\" Katara said. \"That's why we're asking.\"\n\nWhen the others went out to check Appa's harness, Katara leaned against the table. The salt-stained packet from home rested beneath her hand.\n\n\"I'm afraid I'll get there and find out how much I missed,\" she admitted.\n\nI reached for her fingers and let her decide whether to hold them. She did.\n\n\"Then you'll tell me what you want to share,\" I said.\n\nShe drew me closer by the front of my shirt. Her kiss was brief and hungry enough to interrupt the worry for a moment, not erase it.\n\nOutside, Toph called that somebody had put the harness buckle on backwards. Katara laughed against my cheek.\n\n\"We should rescue him,\" I said.\n\n\"No,\" she murmured. \"Let's give him a minute to solve his own problem.\"\n\nThe travel packet stayed on the kitchen table.\n\nI touched it once, feeling the uneven seam beneath my fingers. Katara watched me.\n\n\"What?\" she asked.\n\n\"Your aunt's stitching is crooked.\"\n\n\"She was repairing three fishing nets and a sleeping mat when she made that.\"\n\n\"Then I retract the criticism.\"\n\n\"Wise.\"\n\nShe smiled, but her eyes were wet.\n\nBefore dinner, Katara asked to speak to the clinic's coordinator through the agreed message route. She wanted to know what the staff needed, not just what the letter had frightened her into imagining.\n\nThe answer came back with a practical list. Two local healers were already managing the treatment room. They wanted Katara to demonstrate a few procedures and help revise a supply ledger, then leave them able to do the work without her. The visit was important, but she wasn't being summoned to save people who lacked their own judgment.\n\n\"That is better news than I expected,\" she said.\n\nAang offered to arrange transport but waited for her decision. Toph asked how long Appa would need to rest between the outward and return journeys. Nobody made the itinerary into a competition over who was most helpful.\n\nI looked at our calendar. \"Would you like a quiet hour with me before you go?\"\n\nKatara glanced at the departure time. \"Half an hour. And I want a little time alone to pack the medical notes.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nAang asked whether she'd like company on the first leg. She did. Toph decided to stay and check the bridge inspection rather than come along because she hated missing work she'd actually promised to do. We each had somewhere to be and someone who might miss us.\n\nI was still frightened that the house would feel different when Katara came back. Our relationship was openly polyamorous by choice, and we'd made that agreement because nobody was meant to be trapped in one version of the household. Knowing it didn't remove the ache. It just kept me from treating her trip as disloyalty.\n\nKatara took my hand for a moment. \"You don't have to be cheerful about this.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Good. I wasn't planning to be.\"\n\nThen she went to pack the notes, and we kept our small piece of time before the evening's departure.\n\nWe ate dinner early because Aang wanted to leave before the weather changed. Toph told terrible jokes about snow. Katara pretended not to enjoy them. I packed an extra scarf into her bag and tucked a small folded note beside the medical supplies.\n\nShe found it before we reached the courtyard.\n\nREAD THIS WHEN SOMEONE TELLS YOU THAT YOU HAVE TO BE EVERYWHERE AT ONCE.\n\nShe unfolded it.\n\nYOU ARE ALLOWED TO BE ONE PERSON IN ONE PLACE. WE CAN DO OUR PART HERE.\n\nKatara pressed her lips together.\n\n\"You'll make me cry before I board.\"\n\n\"Then I'll claim it's the wind.\"\n\n\"Awful excuse.\"\n\n\"Very professional.\"\n\nShe kissed me beside the open door. The air was cold, and she tasted faintly of mint tea. Her hands settled at my waist, warm through the fabric. I felt how much I wanted to ask her to stay, and how relieved I was that wanting did not have to become a demand.\n\nWhen she drew back, Aang was checking Appa's saddle.\n\nToph stood beside him, listening to the sky.\n\n\"Weather's coming,\" she said.\n\n\"Can you feel the clouds?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. But Twinkle Toes is making that face.\"\n\nAang laughed.\n\nKatara climbed onto Appa and turned to look down at us.\n\n\"I'll write.\"\n\n\"Don't promise a schedule you can't keep,\" I said.\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"I'll write when I can.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThey rose into the evening, a broad shadow moving across the rooftops. Toph stayed beside me until Appa disappeared beyond the hills.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" she said.\n\n\"Missing her already.\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nShe took my hand.\n\nWe stood there until the cold reached our fingers.\n\nOver the next few days, the house changed shape without her. Aang returned after bringing Katara safely to the South, and the three of us learned where she had quietly handled small things we hadn't noticed. The clinic inventory was missing a signature. The pantry needed water. Someone had to mend the curtain that kept blowing over the stove.\n\nWe did those things.\n\nWhen a letter finally arrived, it carried two pages of Katara's handwriting and a tiny sketch of the treatment room she'd helped repair. The roof had stopped leaking. The beds were full. She was exhausted and happy to have something useful to do.\n\nAt the bottom, she had written:\n\nI MISS YOU. ALL OF YOU. PLEASE DO NOT TURN THE HOUSE INTO A DISASTER WHILE I'M GONE.\n\nToph snorted.\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nAang looked at the pile of laundry he'd promised to fold.\n\nI folded the letter and set it on the table beside four bowls.\n\nOne of them was empty.\n\nIt did not feel like a threat.\n\nIt felt like a place waiting to be used whenever the person who belonged there chose to return.","word_count":1813}}};for(const story of lib.series){const chapters=updates[story.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of story.chapters){const x=chapters[ch.id];if(!x||x.number!==ch.chapter_number)continue;ch.content=x.content;ch.title=x.title;ch.word_count=x.word_count;}}})();
